# ==============================================================================
# External Entry Mail — operator template
# ==============================================================================
# Copy this file to `mail.env` in the Supabase deployment repository
# (system-cloie-infra/supabase/), next to the upstream `docker-compose.yml`.
# This copy stays in git; the filled one never does.
#
#   cp mail.env.example mail.env
#
# Apply it with the overlay in this repository, from the same directory as the
# upstream `docker-compose.yml`:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env --env-file mail.env \
#     -f docker-compose.yml \
#     -f <path-to>/supabase/docker-compose.auth-mail.yml \
#     up -d --force-recreate auth
#
# Both env files are required. `--env-file .env` supplies the base stack's own
# values (POSTGRES_PASSWORD, JWT_SECRET, JWT_EXPIRY, API_EXTERNAL_URL,
# POSTGRES_HOST, POSTGRES_PORT); this file supplies the values the overlay
# maps. Compose applies them in order: overlapping SMTP and origin settings
# intentionally resolve to this file. Keep database/JWT secrets in the base .env.
#
# `--force-recreate` is required: these are container environment variables, and
# `docker compose restart auth` re-runs the container with its ORIGINAL
# environment, silently keeping the previous values.
#
# NEVER put a filled copy in this repository, in a commit, in `.env.example`, or
# in the System CLOIE application deployment. Supabase Auth is the only mail
# sender, and its credentials belong to the infrastructure repository or a
# secrets manager. A filled `mail.env` here is git-ignored but still wrong.
#
# Every required value below is referenced by `docker-compose.auth-mail.yml` with
# Compose's `${VAR:?message}` form, so a missing one aborts `up` before anything
# starts rather than booting an instance that accepts a registration and silently
# swallows the mail. Set MAILER_OTP_EXP explicitly to 600. Other platform policy
# values carry defaults in the overlay and are listed here as the contract.
#
# Cost rule: pick a free tier, never enter a payment method. See
# docs/runbooks/external-entry-mail.md.
# ==============================================================================


# --- 1. Pick one relay, and fill only that block ------------------------------
# Set every value in the chosen block and comment out the others; two live
# relays turn a delivery outage into a second bill.

# Option A — Brevo. Recommended default. Its published Free plan includes 300
# email sends per day with no credit card required, and up to 1,000 additional
# transactional emails are held in a retry queue past the daily ceiling rather
# than delivered or billed. Brevo also puts a "Sent with Brevo" footer on free
# sends. Re-check the current ceiling at the sources in the runbook before
# relying on these numbers.
#   Free-plan limits: https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208580669
#   SMTP keys:       https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/7959631848850
# Create the key at https://app.brevo.com/settings/keys/smtp — the full key is
# displayed exactly once, and a Standard key (64 chars) is the default; the
# 15-char "Short" key exists only for clients that truncate long passwords.
SMTP_HOST="smtp-relay.brevo.com"
SMTP_PORT="587"
SMTP_USER="<<FILL: brevo smtp login, the unique sender e-mail Brevo issued>>"
SMTP_PASS="<<FILL: brevo smtp key>>"
SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL="<<FILL: the From address on a domain you control, e.g. no-reply@system-cloie.app>>"
SMTP_SENDER_NAME="System CLOIE"

# Option B — Resend. Published free tier: 100 transactional emails per day and
# 3,000 per month, and both sent and received (inbound) mail count against the
# quota. Requires a verified domain. Verified 2026-10-04.
#   https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/account-quotas-and-limits
# SMTP_HOST="smtp.resend.com"
# SMTP_PORT="587"
# SMTP_USER="resend"
# SMTP_PASS="<<FILL: resend smtp key>>"
# SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL="<<FILL: the From address on a domain you control>>"
# SMTP_SENDER_NAME="System CLOIE"

# Option C — Mailgun. Requires a verified domain. Confirm the current free
# ceiling in Mailgun's own documentation before relying on a figure.
# SMTP_HOST="smtp.mailgun.org"
# SMTP_PORT="587"
# SMTP_USER="<<FILL: mailgun smtp username, the postmaster address for your domain>>"
# SMTP_PASS="<<FILL: mailgun smtp password>>"
# SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL="<<FILL: the From address on a domain you control>>"
# SMTP_SENDER_NAME="System CLOIE"

# Option D — an ACD Google Workspace mailbox. No DNS work at all, but the mail
# arrives from a Workspace address and is filtered hardest by university
# domains. Fallback only. Use a 16-character Google app password, never the
# account password.
# SMTP_HOST="smtp.gmail.com"
# SMTP_PORT="587"
# SMTP_USER="<<FILL: the Workspace mailbox address>>"
# SMTP_PASS="<<FILL: a 16-character Google app password, not the account password>>"
# SMTP_ADMIN_EMAIL="<<FILL: the Workspace mailbox address>>"
# SMTP_SENDER_NAME="System CLOIE"

# Authenticate your sending domain with the exact records the provider supplies.
# Brevo uses its verification code, DKIM and DMARC records. Preserve existing
# mail DNS; do not add a second SPF record or change MX records for Auth mail.
#   https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/12163873383186


# --- 2. Templates. Fill once, per target deployment ---------------------------
# GoTrue fetches these bodies over HTTP at send time, caches each for
# GOTRUE_MAILER_TEMPLATE_MAX_AGE (10 minutes by default), and treats any
# non-200 response, timeout, or parse failure as a send failure — logged as
# `template_body_http_error`. There is no filesystem-path option, so you must
# serve each file at an HTTP(S) URL the Auth container can reach, and the URL
# must return 200 to an unauthenticated GET.
# Its built-in defaults render a link, not the {{ .Token }} the entry form
# needs, so a correct send can still be unusable. Canonical files are served
# by the System CLOIE application at /auth-email/:
#   public/auth-email/confirm_signup.html
#   public/auth-email/recovery.html
# Keep {{ .Token }} in both. Replace only the application origin below.
MAILER_TEMPLATES_CONFIRMATION="<<FILL: HTTPS application origin>>/auth-email/confirm_signup.html"
MAILER_TEMPLATES_RECOVERY="<<FILL: HTTPS application origin>>/auth-email/recovery.html"
MAILER_SUBJECTS_CONFIRMATION="Your System CLOIE verification code"
MAILER_SUBJECTS_RECOVERY="Your System CLOIE password recovery code"


# --- 3. Origins. Fill once, per target deployment -----------------------------
# The System CLOIE origin for this target. Becomes GOTRUE_SITE_URL and the
# redirect allowlist. List exact callback and code-page URLs for every approved
# application origin, without public wildcard hosts. For example:
# https://app.example.edu.ph/api/auth/callback,
# https://app.example.edu.ph/verify-email,https://app.example.edu.ph/reset-password
SITE_URL="<<FILL: https origin of this System CLOIE deployment>>"
ADDITIONAL_REDIRECT_URLS="<<FILL: comma-separated exact callback, verify-email and reset-password URLs>>"


# --- 4. Email/password and mail switches --------------------------------------
# Email signup on, autoconfirm off. Autoconfirm would let an unverified address
# reach domain linkage, and it also suppresses the verification mail entirely.
ENABLE_EMAIL_SIGNUP="true"
ENABLE_EMAIL_AUTOCONFIRM="false"

# Ten minutes, in seconds. One value governs EVERY emailed code — confirmation,
# recovery, invite, magic link, reauthentication and both email-change senders —
# and verification recomputes it per request. Upstream Compose has no mapping
# for it, so an instance that never receives it runs at GoTrue's own
# 86400-second default.
#
# Shortening it invalidates codes already in flight, with no grace period. After
# a restart that sets this to 600, a code sent nine minutes earlier has one
# minute left, not ten. Announce the window before restarting Auth on a live
# target.
MAILER_OTP_EXP="600"

# Six-digit codes, matching the entry form's code field.
MAILER_OTP_LENGTH="6"

# Per-address mail cooldown. This variable is SMTP_MAX_FREQUENCY, not
# MAILER_MAX_FREQUENCY — the container key is GOTRUE_SMTP_MAX_FREQUENCY. There
# is no GOTRUE_SECURITY_MAX_AGE to clamp the OTP lifetime against; that name
# does not exist in Supabase Auth. Do not lower this below the app's 60-second
# resend cooldown.
SMTP_MAX_FREQUENCY="1m"

# The application schema requires 8 (src/lib/schemas/external-entry.ts).
# GoTrue's own floor is 6, so a lower instance value would let Auth accept a
# password the application then rejects.
PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH="8"

# Require reauthentication for older sessions changing passwords, matching
# config.toml's secure_password_change. Confirm email changes on both old and
# new addresses, matching double_confirm_changes. Workspace confinement is
# separately enforced by the application.
SECURE_PASSWORD_CHANGE="true"
MAILER_SECURE_EMAIL_CHANGE="true"

# Backstops. RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT gates outbound mail before the relay is
# contacted; it is a bound, not a guarantee — 20 per hour is a ceiling of up to
# 480 in a day if every hour were used, which is above a 300/day free tier.
# RATE_LIMIT_OTP gates the send endpoints per client IP; code verification is
# not governed by it.
RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL_SENT="20"
RATE_LIMIT_OTP="30"

# The relay block above already carries SMTP_SENDER_NAME. Do not repeat it
# here; a duplicated key resolves by last-one-wins and hides a stale value.


# --- 5. ACD Google sign-in is NOT set here ------------------------------------
# Every public entrance offers Google alongside email/password, but Google is
# configured on the base Compose file, not by this overlay: `docker-compose.auth-mail.yml`
# deliberately touches no `GOTRUE_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_*` key, so a target that
# already serves Google keeps serving it.
#
# To turn Google on for a target, uncomment the matching `GOTRUE_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_*`
# lines in the base `docker-compose.yml` alongside the entries below in
# upstream `.env.example`, and register `${API_EXTERNAL_URL}/callback` as an
# authorized redirect URI on the Google Cloud OAuth client. Google sign-in is
# an account-link path, not a mail path: a Google-only sign-in sends nothing,
# so none of the settings above depend on it.
#   https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials


# --- 6. Application deployment (NOT this file, NOT the Supabase resource) ----
# Set this single value in the System CLOIE application resource so recovery
# links are deterministic behind the proxy. No other application value is
# involved in mail, and none should be added. See `.env.example`, which
# describes the application-only environment contract.
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="<<FILL: same origin as SITE_URL above>>"


# ==============================================================================
# Checks. Run both before `up -d --force-recreate`.
# ==============================================================================
#
# 1. Leftover placeholders. Inspects only uncommented assignments, so the
#    comment lines above are not self-matching. Prints every unfilled token and
#    exits non-zero while any remain.
#
#   grep -nE '^[[:space:]]*[A-Z0-9_]+=.*<<FILL' mail.env \
#     && echo "STOP: placeholders remain" || echo "ready"
#
# 2. Fail-fast resolution. Validate interpolation without printing or writing
#    the resolved configuration, which contains SMTP, database and JWT secrets.
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env --env-file mail.env \
#     -f docker-compose.yml \
#     -f <path-to>/supabase/docker-compose.auth-mail.yml \
#     config --quiet
